  "summary": "The third proposal's 'reuse final-human-approval' approach has four critical problems that undermine its claimed simplicity. First, the approve and reject handlers are hard-coded for post-implementation semantics: approve moves to 'done' and reject moves to 'implementing' with impl_review feedback. For pre-implementation gated items, rejection should route to 'plan-inbox' or 'ideas', not 'implementing', and approval should route to 'approved', not 'done'. Fixing both the TUI handler (tui.py ~line 2904) and the WebUI move handler (tui.py ~line 2176) to branch on pre-impl vs post-impl means the actual change is 40-60 lines across 3+ files, not the claimed '~15 lines'. Second, using 'impl_spec is empty' as the heuristic to distinguish pre-impl from post-impl holds is fragile — if spec-writing partially ran and failed before the item was escalated to final-human-approval, the item would have a partial impl_spec and be misrouted to 'done' on approval. A proper approach needs an explicit metadata field like 'human_gate_reason: pre-impl | post-impl | max-retries' rather than inferring state from data completeness. Third, overloading a single phase for two fundamentally different hold reasons creates UX confusion in the board view: the WebUI and TUI both show items as a list of titles in stage columns, and users scanning final-human-approval cannot distinguish 'needs human sign-off before implementation starts' from 'implementation complete, needs final review' without clicking into each item. This defeats the purpose of a kanban-style board. Fourth, the proposal ignores the interaction with the existing max-retry escalation: phases.py already moves items to final-human-approval when plan review fails 6+ times or exhausts retry attempts. If a gated item also ends up in final-human-approval due to flag-based routing, the approve handler cannot reliably determine whether the human is approving a clean plan or accepting a plan that failed review — both would look identical (empty impl_spec, sitting in final-human-approval). The cleaner architectural direction is a dedicated pre-implementation gate phase (e.g., 'human-gate') with its own handlers that route approval to 'approved' and rejection to 'plan-inbox', keeping final-human-approval exclusively for post-implementation review. This adds one more entry to STAGES but eliminates all the conditional branching, heuristic detection, and semantic overloading problems."
